  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe adopt a multi-resolution, population-based optimization strategy informed by Bayesian optimization and iterated local search to escape local optima. The method consists of:\n- Bayesian global optimization at low resolutions with automatic hyperparameter tuning\n- Progressive pattern refinement via topological morphing between resolution levels\n- Local optimization with adaptive projection constraints\n- Targeted search in promising regions using stochastic dominance models\n\nKey improvements over previous approaches:\n- Hybridizes global Bayesian exploration with targeted local refinement\n- Adapts optimization complexity based on the problem's multi-resolution nature\n- Uses stochastic topological transforms to escape local optima\n- Maintains rigorous convex constraints through adaptive projection schemes\n\nThe primary limitation is computational scaling, but we optimize sequentially with budget control.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Piecewise constant step function with n-point discretization over [0,2]\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(i) \u2264 1, \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n- Objective: minimize C\u2085 = max_{k} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over [0,2-k]\n- Optimization formulation: minimize c\u2085 via projected gradient descent with population-based surrogate\n- Surrogate model: Gaussian Process regression to approximate correlation extremes\n- Transition between resolutions: Recursive resampling with minimal topological perturbation\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nWe implement the Stochastic Morphing Optimization (SMO) algorithm:\n\nPreparation:\n- Initialize parents from supplied values if available\n- Define resolution hierarchy: R = [64, 128, 256, 512] \n\nAlgorithm:\ndef smo_optimization(n, target_sum):\n    # Initialize population with resampled parent if n=256\n    popsize = 40\n    pop = [resample_vec(initial_h_values) if n==256 else project_h(np.random.rand(n), target_sum) for _ in range(popsize)]\n    bounds = [(0,1)]*n\n\n    # Bayesian global optimization stage:\n    gp = GaussianProcessRegressor()\n    max_iter = 200\n    for it in range(max_iter):\n        # Evaluate candidates\n        vals = [compute_c5(x) for x in pop]\n        # Update GP regression\n        X = np.array(range(len(pop)))[:,None]\n        gp.fit(X, vals)\n        # Predict next best point\n        next_idx = my_acquisition_function(gp, X, vals) % len(pop)\n        # Local refinement\n        new_x = local_refine(pop[next_idx], n, target_sum)\n        new_x = project_h(new_x, target_sum)\n        vals.append(compute_c5(new_x))\n        pop.append(new_x)\n        # Elitism: keep best candidates\n        paired = [(compute_c5(x), x) for x in pop]\n        paired.sort(key=lambda z: z[0])\n        pop = [x for (c,x) in paired[:popsize]]\n\n    # Progressive transition stage:\n    if n==256:\n        for next_n in [512, 1024]:\n            # Topological morph via interpolation\n            morph_factor = 0.3 * (64 - resolve_level)\n            new_pop = []\n            for x in pop[:5]:\n                new_x = interpolate_transition(x, morph_factor)\n                new_x = project_h(new_x, next_n/2.0)\n                new_pop.append(new_x)\n            pop = new_pop\n            # Local optimization of morphed population\n            polished = []\n            for x in pop:\n                x_opt = scpo.optimize(x, next_n, target_sum, n_iter=100)\n                polished.append(x_opt)\n            pop = polished\n            n = next_n\n            target_sum = n/2.0\n\n    return best_candidate\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n- Optimize with population size 40 and 200 iterations for each resolution\n- Bayesian acquisition = Expected Improvement (EI)\n- Local optimization uses SLSQP with constraints\n- Topological morphing parameter varies by resolution level\n- Morphing interpolation: Sinc interpolation for step function components\n- Progressive resolution strategy: R = [64,128,256,512]\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n- GE brute-force: n=1024 requires ~3e6 evaluations\n- Gaussian Process reduces to ~10% evaluations\n- Progressive resolution ensures feasibility across scales\n- Budget allocation: 40% for initial exploration, 30% for final polish\n- Early termination: M\u00f6bius test for improvement stalls\n- Fallback: Maintain best candidate from previous resolution\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    initialize optimizers and metrics\n    if 'initial_h_values' in kwargs and initial_h_values is not None:\n        start_vec = np.array(initial_h_values)\n    else:\n        start_vec = np.clip(np.random.rand(128),0,1) with adjusted sum\n    \n    best_c5 = compute_c5(start_vec)\n    best_vec = start_vec\n    # Dynamic budget apportionment by resolution\n    return best_vec, best_c5, n  # with candidate resampled to starting length\n\nStates that the candidate must satisfy:\n- 0 \u2264 h(i) \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u222bh(x)dx = 1 (within 1e-6 tolerance)\n- Smooth transition between resolution levels\n\nNumerical checks are verified by finite difference correlation and integral approximation.\n\nBuilt-in fallback: revert to parent solution upon initialization failure.",
